            <ArticleTitle>Giant coronary aneurysms developed in a child of Kawasaki disease with only 3 days of fever.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>339-41</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1017/S104795111000017X</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Prolonged fever, 5 days or more, is the cardinal feature of Kawasaki disease. We described a 5-year-old boy with initial presentations of fever, conjunctivitis, and strawberry tongue. The fever only lasted for 3 days. However, giant coronary aneurysms developed later. This patient reminds us that coronary complications could happen even the fever is less than 5 days. Criteria of fever duration might need reconsideration.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
